Consider an arbitrary element $x \in \SL_2(F)$with order 2. That is $x^2 = I_{\SL_2(F)}$, $x 
eq I_{\SL_2(F)}$and thus $x=x^{-1}$.  egin{equation*} x =  egin{bmatrix} \alpha &  eta \ \gamma & \delta \end{bmatrix} =  egin{bmatrix} \alpha &  eta \ \gamma & \delta \end{bmatrix}^{-1} =  egin{bmatrix} \delta & -  eta \ - \gamma & \alpha \end{bmatrix}. \end{equation*} 
oindent Thus $\alpha = \delta$, $ eta = -  eta \Rightarrow 2 eta = 0$ and $\gamma = - \gamma \Rightarrow 2\gamma = 0$. In the case of $p 
eq 2$ this gives $ eta = 0 = \gamma$. So  egin{equation*} x =  egin{bmatrix} \alpha & 0 \ 0 & \alpha \end{bmatrix}. \end{equation*} 
oindent Also $\alpha^2 = 1$ since $x \in$ $\SL_2(F)$, so $\alpha = \pm 1$. For $x$ to have order 2, we must have $\alpha = - 1$. Hence there is a unique element of order 2, namely $- I_{\SL_2(F)}$.
